          "text": "A CUDA graph replay is faster than eager kernel launches primarily because it eliminates the CPU-side overhead associated with the CUDA driver and runtime. When you launch kernels eagerly, the CPU must perform a significant amount of work for every single kernel: it validates parameters, allocates resources, and communicates with the driver to schedule the work. This creates a bottleneck where the GPU often sits idle waiting for the CPU to catch up, especially when kernels are small or numerous. By capturing a sequence of operations into a graph, the driver pre-validates the entire sequence and optimizes the scheduling logic. When the graph is replayed, the CPU simply issues a single command to execute the pre-compiled structure, allowing the GPU to launch kernels back-to-back with minimal latency.\n\nFurthermore, graphs allow for better optimization of the GPU's internal scheduling. Because the driver knows the entire dependency chain of the graph upfront, it can optimize memory transfers and kernel scheduling more aggressively than it can with individual, isolated launches. This reduces the \"launch gap\"\u2014the time between one kernel finishing and the next starting\u2014resulting in higher GPU utilization. Essentially, the graph acts as a pre-compiled script that the GPU can execute with high efficiency, bypassing the repetitive interpretation and validation steps required by eager execution.\n\nHowever,",
